  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe heat will continue this week. Ensembles show a decreasing trend \nin daytime 850mb dew points over the second half of the week, a \nsignal of moisture mixing out more. However, air temperatures also \ntrend upwards during the same period, so maximum daily heat indices \nmay continue to stay above 105F for some areas. Overnight \ntemperatures are also expected to also rise slightly towards the end \nof the week, with the hottest areas (urban I-35 corridor and the \nlower plains) not spending much time below 80F. \n\nEntering the weekend, the large subtropical ridge responsible for \nshaping the hot and dry weather this week will shift west. The \nrecent model trend has moved farther west with some ensemble means \nplacing the core of the ridge as far west as Arizona/Nevada by \nSaturday. Northerly flow on the ridge's eastern flanks could bring a \nweak front into our area Saturday or Sunday. This brings a potential \nfor isolated rains over the weekend. Rain amounts at this range \ndon't look especially impressive given that the front is favored to \ncontinue south at a steady pace, but a few thunderstorms are \npossible. A few degrees of cooling are possible early next week, an \noutcome slightly more favored by the EPS/AIFS, but the overall \npicture stays hot as is typical for this time of year.",
